text:
nina: Hit a parity bug while testing the quickstart websocket demo. The REST API supports server-sent chunked events for /v1/streams but the Node SDK streaming client buffers the whole response and only emits at end. That makes the demo feel extremely laggy.

arjun: Oof. Is this the same as the previously reported streaming-timing gap? Which client are you using?

nina: Using `@redwood/sdk@2.4.1` and the sampleapp `realtime-chat` that subscribes to `/v1/streams/message_stream`. The server yields: `event: chunk\ndata: {"delta":"hi"}\n` repeatedly. REST client shows immediate deltas but SDK waits until connection close.

dave: That's a known oversight: our HTTP client wrapper for Node currently accumulates chunks because we standardized on fetch-like API that returns full body. We did add a streaming helper in Python but didn't expose a low-level stream reader in Node SDK.

arjun: Can you paste the minimal repro from sampleapp?

nina: Sure:```js
// sampleapp subscription
const stream = await sdk.streams.subscribe({ stream: 'message_stream' })
stream.on('data', chunk => console.log('delta', chunk))
// only logs once at the end instead of streaming continuously
```

sam: Product impact: realtime onboarding tutorial depends on chunked output to show types as customers type. If SDK doesn't stream, features that rely on progressive output (like token-level latency metrics) are broken.

dave: Proposed fixes: 1) expose a `streams.subscribeRaw()` that returns an EventSource/ReadableStream wrapper so callers can listen to incremental chunks; 2) add a compatibility test in CI that compares REST streaming vs SDK streaming behavior for a small prompt and asserts first-chunk < 200ms.

arjun: I prefer option 1 for immediate patch. We should keep the higher-level `subscribe()` as-is but mark it streaming-capable once underlying implementation is fixed. Also update docs to not claim parity until the test is green.

nina: I'll add a fallback in the sampleapp that uses EventSource directly if `sdk.streams.subscribeRaw` is missing. Snippet:```js
if (!sdk.streams || !sdk.streams.subscribeRaw) {
  const es = new EventSource(`${API_BASE}/v1/streams/message_stream`, { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${TOKEN}` } })
  es.onmessage = e => { const data = JSON.parse(e.data); showDelta(data.delta) }
} else {
  const s = await sdk.streams.subscribeRaw({ stream: 'message_stream' })
  for await (const chunk of s) { showDelta(chunk.delta) }
}
```

dave: I'll open a Node SDK PR implementing subscribeRaw using native http request and streaming parser. Will include unit test that simulates chunked responses and ensures data events emit immediately.

arjun: Also add an integration smoke test in the quickstart workflow so the sampleapp run in CI verifies progressive streaming. If it fails we should block the release.

devex-bot: Created issue #467: "Node SDK: streaming parity - add subscribeRaw for chunked server streams" assigned to @dave. https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/issues/467

nina: Thanks — deploying the sampleapp EventSource fallback now so demos are smooth while SDK PR lands.

dave: PR opened: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/pull/610 implementing subscribeRaw + tests. Requesting review from @arjun and @sam.

sam: Nice. After PR merges let's update the SDK changelog and the quickstart README to point at the streaming guide.
